 {"id":"fabro-b887","title":"merge-upstream: reuse a parked reviewed merge branch instead of re-merging from scratch","status":"open","type":"feature","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-06T13:37:52.764Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-07T13:23:42.740Z","description":"Observed 2026-09-06 (run 01M1V7XSV75B): ~50 min of merge+gate+review work (commit 401e3ceb7, reviewer approved all 13 touchpoints) parked on the run branch because the routing label missed (fabro-de4d). The next pass would REDO the entire merge — repeated nightly, each redo burns 40-60 min LLM for identical, already-reviewed output. Wanted (defense in depth beyond de4d): (a) the merge-upstream graph opens the run PR as PART of the approved exit path (before/independent of the final routing), so an approved merge always reaches auto-merge even if later routing parks; and/or (b) the merge stage's prompt starts by checking for an existing parked run branch of the same workflow whose reviewer approved (checkpoint/event evidence) and reuses/promotes that branch (PR) instead of re-merging. Either way: approved work never silently repeats.","labels":["workflows","engine"],"assignee":"fabro"}
 {"id":"fabro-dd7e","title":"engine: automation-spawned conductor runs carry no automation_id — overlap guard is blind, scheduler stacks parallel passes","status":"closed","type":"bug","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-06T19:02:15.182Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-06T19:09:18.411Z","description":"ROOT CAUSE CORRECTED (2026-09-06, investigation): the automation tag IS persisted — scheduler-spawned conductor runs carry automation {id: conductor, trigger_id: schedule} in RunCreated (verified on 01M1VZR3AAWY and stacked pass 01M1W1EZ5WZ; second stacked pass 01M1W1HHCE showed None — CHECK why, possibly different creation path). The real gap: the conductor automation had on_overlap: None (= default Fire), so the existing fabro-09ea skip-guard was never consulted — three passes stacked while one ran. IMMEDIATE OPS FIX APPLIED: PUT replaceAutomation set on_overlap=skip (revision c9b0258770025888) — the guard now suppresses fires while a pass is non-terminal. REMAINING WORK for the line: (1) verify the next fire slot logs 'Scheduled fire skipped: overlapping run still non-terminal' while a pass runs; (2) investigate why pass 01M1W1HHCE lacks the automation tag (creation path audit); (3) consider making Skip the DEFAULT for scheduled automations (or at least require an explicit choice at creation) so unattended lines cannot stack by configuration oversight — small engine change + test; (4) surface on_overlap in the automations web UI.","labels":["engine","workflows"],"closedAt":"2026-09-06T19:09:18.411Z","closeReason":"Primary defect was configuration, not code: 09ea's skip-guard exists and runs are tagged; the conductor automation just never had on_overlap=skip set. Fixed live via API (revision c9b0258770025888). The remaining engineering follow-ups ((2) untagged 01M1W1HHCE creation-path audit, (3) Skip-as-default + test, (4) UI surfacing) are re-filed as the updated description for a fresh seed by the revisor/line — see description."}
 {"id":"fabro-81b7","title":"develop: implementer (and reviewer) must load and validate against the rust-style-guide skill","status":"open","type":"task","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-06T19:04:15.238Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-07T13:23:42.839Z","description":"User review 2026-09-06: does the develop implementer use the rust-style-guide? Facts: (1) ACCESS EXISTS — run sandboxes discover the skill (agent.skills.discovered lists rust-style-guide from /workspace/fabro/.fabro/skills); (2) the implementer prompt NEVER mentions it (no occurrence of style.guide in .fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/implementer.md); neither does the reviewer prompt — the binding Rust policy is invisible to the whole develop pipeline. The 2026-08-27 fabro_run_logs lesson (agent-side): a skipped guide load shipped two logic bugs that a guide-first pass would have caught at design time; the local iterate skill got a mechanical gate for exactly this — the develop workflow needs the same. Wanted: (a) implementer prompt: BEFORE the first Rust edit, read the skill's guidelines.md index and the pages covering the diff (name the pages in the summary as proof-of-work); validate the implementation against them; deviations are findings, not accidents; (b) reviewer prompt: the standards axis IS the rust-style-guide — load workflows/code-review-refactor.md plus the pages covering the diff under review; (c) keep it cheap: pages-on-demand only, no full-guide reads. Basis: implementer.md/reviewer.md grep (zero mentions), sandbox skill discovery events (e.g. merge run 01M1V7XSV75B seq 25), iterate-skill mechanical-gate precedent, fabro_run_logs postmortem.","labels":["workflows","revision"],"assignee":"fabro"}
-{"id":"fabro-fb16","title":"engine: make Skip the safe default for scheduled automations + audit the untagged scheduler run path + surface on_overlap in the web UI","status":"in_progress","type":"task","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-06T19:09:33.140Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-07T16:33:38.083Z","description":"\n\nUSER-DIRECTIVE EVIDENCE (2026-09-07, user in chat): this seed is HIGH priority - without Skip as the safe default the serialized line keeps hitting duplicate-pass incidents (see the 2026-09-07 08:00 incident above), and UI-overwrites make manual repairs futile: apps/fabro-web/app/routes/automations-edit.tsx replaceAutomation payload OMITS on_overlap (the form model has no overlap field at all), so ANY save from the web UI resets the policy to the Fire default - the API-set skip from the 08:00 repair would be silently reverted by the next UI edit. The UI dialog must carry the on_overlap switch (edit AND create), and the server-side default for schedule triggers must become Skip so untagged automations can never fire overlapping passes again.","labels":["engine","workflows","web"],"assignee":"fabro"}
